  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 1220 PM EST Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and seasonably mild through mid-week. \n\nTemperatures as of 12 PM have warmed into the mid-upper 60s for\nmost of the area. Afternoon highs in the upper 60s to lower 70s\nare expected under clear skies with a light SW wind. Not as \ncold tonight with lows in the low-mid 40s for most. Another \nseasonably mild day is on the way for Wednesday with sky cover \nand temperatures very similar to today. However, unlike today, \nclouds will likely gradually increase in coverage late in the \nday as low pressure begins to take shape well NW of the region.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A strong cold front brings chances for showers \nThursday-Friday, but any potential for appreciable rain is low. Much \ncooler temperatures follow behind the front with below freezing \ntemperatures likely inland.\n\nA big change remains on the horizon as we approach late week. A \nshortwave trough is forecast to dive southward out of the north-\ncentral CONUS Wednesday, with a larger longwave trough and embedded \nupper low setting up shop over most of the eastern CONUS by Thursday \nand Friday. While isolated to scattered showers are possible \nThursday and Friday, confidence is decreasing. PoPs have lowered to \n25-40% along and north of I-64 and less than 25% south of I-64 on \nThursday and 15-20% only across the Eastern Shore on Friday. The 12z \nGEFS only has a 20-40% chance for greater than 0.1\" of rain with \nnegligible probs for greater than 0.5\" of rain. Meanwhile, the \n6z EPS is similar with generally a 30-50% chance for greater \nthan 0.1\" of rain and negligible probs for greater than 0.5\". As\nsuch, the main focus will be the much cooler temperatures that \noverspread the area behind the associated frontal passage as the\nanomalously strong trough moves into the region. Thursday looks\nto be the last mild day for a while with pre-frontal highs in \nthe mid-upper 60s for most and around 70F SE. A series of cold \nfronts then crosses the local area Thursday evening and \novernight, bringing much cooler temps by Friday. At this time, \nforecast highs Friday are only in the 50s. The large upper \ntrough remains parked over most of the eastern CONUS Saturday, \nand this still looks like the coolest day of the period. Highs \nSat are only expected to reach the lower 50s for most of the \narea with upper 40s possible across the far northern portions of\nthe area (especially the lower MD Eastern Shore). These cool \ntemps continue into early next week. Overnight lows will also be\nmuch cooler with lows in the upper 20s to around freezing for \nmost locations away from the coast Friday, Saturday, and Sunday \nnights. Subfreezing temperatures may continue (for lows) Monday \nand Tuesday nights as well. As such, the growing season looks to\nbe ending shortly for most areas. Will likely need a Freeze \nWatch as we get closer to the weekend. Gusty NW winds could make\nthese temps feel even chillier.",
